  "content": {
    "id": "p11-q10",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Consider the polynomial equation $x^4-10x^3+34x^2-42x+9=0$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p11-q10-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $x=3$ is a double root of the equation.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(3)=P'(3)=0$ and $P''(3)=-4\\ne0$, so $x=3$ is a double root.",
          "worked": "Let $P(x)=x^4-10x^3+34x^2-42x+9$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(3)&=81-270+306-126+9=0\\\\P'(x)&=4x^3-30x^2+68x-42\\\\P'(3)&=108-270+204-42=0\\\\P''(x)&=12x^2-60x+68\\\\P''(3)&=108-180+68=-4\\ne0\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore $x=3$ has multiplicity exactly $2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 33
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q10-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence solve the equation.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=3$ (double root), $x=2\\pm\\sqrt3$.",
          "worked": "Let the remaining roots be $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$. Using the sum and product of the four roots:\n$$\\begin{align*}3+3+\\alpha+\\beta&=10\\\\\\alpha+\\beta&=4\\\\3\\times3\\times\\alpha\\beta&=9\\\\\\alpha\\beta&=1\\end{align*}$$\nThe remaining roots satisfy\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2-4x+1&=0\\\\x&=\\frac{4\\pm\\sqrt{16-4}}2\\\\x&=2\\pm\\sqrt3.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $x=3$ (double root), $2+\\sqrt3$ or $2-\\sqrt3$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      }
    ]
  },
